    ('c0d78581-207b-4223-bcdf-680bd82bce83'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near the southern shore of Strangford Lough, the Audleystown Court Tomb opens a court and a gallery at each end of its cairn. After its 1952 excavation, the galleries were filled with sand.', 'A Neolithic dual court tomb from 3900-3500 BC with two opposing ceremonial courtyards. The remains of at least 34 individuals were found inside its chambers.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c0d78581-207b-4223-bcdf-680bd82bce83:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A Neolithic dual court tomb from 3900-3500 BC with two opposing ceremonial courtyards. The remains of at least 34 individuals were found inside its chambers.'' restored on Audleystown Court Tomb', 'cb412dfc1e8ea85a988f32e69c4981bf95341da82be5865c05147a1ab61755e9|9264c5a78153435de1fd1a2179882a4a33961154da18f9c702441132e0548c88|9264c5a78153435de1fd1a2179882a4a33961154da18f9c702441132e0548c88', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near the southern shore of Strangford Lough, the Audleystown Court Tomb opens a court and a gallery at each end of its cairn. After its 1952 excavation, the galleries were filled with sand."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the tomb lies near the southern shore of Strangford Lough [S1]; Audleystown Court Tomb has a court and a gallery at each end of its cairn [S3, S4]; the tomb was excavated in 1952 [S5]; after the 1952 excavation the galleries were filled with sand [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-066 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Audleystown Court Tomb is a court tomb [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audleystown_Court_Tomb]; It is near the southern shore of Strangford Lough [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audleystown_Court_Tomb]; It has a court and a gallery at each end of its cairn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audleystown_Court_Tomb]; It was excavated in 1952 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audleystown_Court_Tomb]; After the excavation the galleries were filled with sand [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Audleystown_Court_Tomb]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c17927cb-4b63-4fa0-9c64-b72a65decaaa'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'One of the three ancient city-states of Rhodes, founded according to myth by Danaus. Its dramatic acropolis holds a temple to Athena Lindia rebuilt after a fire in 342 BC.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c17927cb-4b63-4fa0-9c64-b72a65decaaa:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''One of the three ancient city-states of Rhodes, founded according to myth by Danaus. Its dramatic acropolis holds a temple to Athena Lindia rebuilt after a fire in 342 BC.'' restored on Lindos', '||7d41e378a59967ef66932f509a3d28258f5ffa26b5f446aaa9cbf3c0afe2a368', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: unproven-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check could not prove: Lindos is an ancient site with an acropolis, on Rhodes, Greece (central claim: a settlement/sanctuary site in Greece); The web check could not prove: Lindos is an archaeological site (with acropolis) on Rhodes, Greece"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c2d8b523-cc72-4987-bc2c-99fd5e5af3c2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Some 4 kilometres southwest of Ivaylovgrad lies Villa Armira, a 1st-century Roman villa on two floors, with 22 separate rooms on the ground floor alone and a panoramic terrace.', 'Villa Armira had 22 separate rooms on the ground floor alone in addition to a panoramic terrace.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c2d8b523-cc72-4987-bc2c-99fd5e5af3c2:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Villa Armira had 22 separate rooms on the ground floor alone in addition to a panoramic terrace.'' restored on Villa Armira', '1b110c8ad217ac820c5b602a4a51b90e7d8252b8b98f03ae87c17e7a56b9f915|26dca55f16b9d7fa91ca80a6778fb5bd200213bc0f3d77a14ddbd161c02694da|26dca55f16b9d7fa91ca80a6778fb5bd200213bc0f3d77a14ddbd161c02694da', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Some 4 kilometres southwest of Ivaylovgrad lies Villa Armira, a 1st-century Roman villa on two floors, with 22 separate rooms on the ground floor alone and a panoramic terrace."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Villa Armira lies some 4 kilometres southwest of Ivaylovgrad [S2]; it is a 1st-century Roman villa [S1]; the villa has two floors [S4]; it had 22 separate rooms on the ground floor alone [S5]; it had a panoramic terrace [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-067 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Villa Armira is a 1st-century Roman villa (central claim) in Bulgaria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Armira]; It lies some 4 km southwest of Ivaylovgrad [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Armira]; The villa has two floors [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Armira]; It had 22 separate rooms on the ground floor alone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Armira]; It has a panoramic terrace [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Armira]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c40df04b-563c-45b1-80db-82e646445531'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the crest of the Berkshire Downs, a dig in 1871 found a cist grave walled with flint on the south side of the Segsbury Camp rampart. Finds included a shield boss and human bones.', 'Excavation at the site by Dr Phené in 1871 discovered a cist grave on the south side of the hillfort rampart.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c40df04b-563c-45b1-80db-82e646445531:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Excavation at the site by Dr Phené in 1871 discovered a cist grave on the south side of the hillfort rampart.'' restored on Segsbury Camp', '0985dfa892c0fae58f409c5d74d3e627a0d3930a0ca552cf471c5d6bbd1e0093|8d903bcd8c911371d60d92c7f2a9ba484de3671fbe0ffbc43268d33bc7c8e1de|8d903bcd8c911371d60d92c7f2a9ba484de3671fbe0ffbc43268d33bc7c8e1de', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the crest of the Berkshire Downs, a dig in 1871 found a cist grave walled with flint on the south side of the Segsbury Camp rampart. Finds included a shield boss and human bones."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Segsbury Camp is on the crest of the Berkshire Downs [S1]; an excavation took place in 1871 [S3]; the dig found a cist grave on the south side of the Segsbury Camp rampart [S3]; the grave was walled with flint [S4]; the finds included a shield boss [S5]; the finds included human bones [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-067 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Segsbury Camp is a hillfort (rampart) on the crest of the Berkshire Downs, England (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segsbury_Camp]; A dig in 1871 found a cist grave on the south side of the rampart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segsbury_Camp]; The cist grave lay on the south side of the rampart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segsbury_Camp]; The excavation took place in 1871 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segsbury_Camp]; The cist grave was walled with flint [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segsbury_Camp]; Finds included a shield boss [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segsbury_Camp]; Finds included human bones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segsbury_Camp]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c4bb47e2-6e2e-49e6-a17b-e9c2ad9c9129'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Today only four upright stones of the original nine remain at Templebryan Stone Circle, The Druid''s Temple. Nearby, a small ruined church stands beside a stone with a bullaun.', 'A small Neolithic stone circle also known as The Druid''s Temple. An Early Christian site lies nearby, layering pagan and Christian sacred space.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c4bb47e2-6e2e-49e6-a17b-e9c2ad9c9129:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "A small Neolithic stone circle also known as The Druid''s Temple. An Early Christian site lies nearby, layering pagan and Christian sacred space." restored on Templebryan Stone Circle', 'ad0a398f5b3cb2edb49bbfff2ed9120e35075c903e7e9dc8962c710d210f0e23|824f4aaac1f9ae8bee37ede31960004370813e7f054aa48a30e748488d1d2c3c|824f4aaac1f9ae8bee37ede31960004370813e7f054aa48a30e748488d1d2c3c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Today only four upright stones of the original nine remain at Templebryan Stone Circle, The Druid''s Temple. Nearby, a small ruined church stands beside a stone with a bullaun."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "today only four upright stones remain at Templebryan Stone Circle [S3]; the circle originally had nine stones [S3]; the circle is also known as The Druid''s Temple [S1]; nearby stands a small ruined church [S4]; the church stands beside a stone with a bullaun [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-067 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Templebryan Stone Circle (also called The Druid''s Temple) is a stone circle in Ireland (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Templebryan_Stone_Circle]; Only four upright stones of the original nine remain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Templebryan_Stone_Circle]; Four upright stones remain today [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Templebryan_Stone_Circle]; The original circle had nine upright stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Templebryan_Stone_Circle]; The church is small [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Templebryan_Stone_Circle]; Nearby is a small ruined church [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Templebryan_Stone_Circle]; The church stands beside a stone with a bullaun [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Templebryan_Stone_Circle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c4ec69f3-aed5-4c87-8a94-808639ab5544'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the same hill range as the Uffington White Horse, close to The Ridgeway, lies Wayland''s Smithy, an Early Neolithic chambered long barrow believed completed around 3430 BCE.', 'A chambered long barrow from ~3600 BC, named after a mythical blacksmith god. Legend says if you leave a horse and a coin overnight, the horse will be shod by morning.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c4ec69f3-aed5-4c87-8a94-808639ab5544:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A chambered long barrow from ~3600 BC, named after a mythical blacksmith god. Legend says if you leave a horse and a coin overnight, the horse will be shod by morning.'' restored on Wayland''s Smithy', '2a10099455edc50f70497b51ab3d0ecf4448df8d8354889582082cfdee63bf2a|9cd7141a6a595d90f7d61a8e35fe51d43a183802bcc290f123e5ebe47ba20dbb|9cd7141a6a595d90f7d61a8e35fe51d43a183802bcc290f123e5ebe47ba20dbb', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the same hill range as the Uffington White Horse, close to The Ridgeway, lies Wayland''s Smithy, an Early Neolithic chambered long barrow believed completed around 3430 BCE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wayland''s Smithy is on the same hill range as the Uffington White Horse [S5]; it is close to The Ridgeway [S5]; it is an Early Neolithic chambered long barrow [S1]; it is believed to have been completed around 3430 BCE [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-067 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wayland''s Smithy is an Early Neolithic chambered long barrow (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayland%27s_Smithy]; The barrow is believed to have been completed around 3430 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayland%27s_Smithy]; It is on the same hill range as the Uffington White Horse [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayland%27s_Smithy]; It is close to The Ridgeway [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayland%27s_Smithy]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c6bd2d66-f54d-4d02-8b14-041414043cf3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Ladle Hill is perhaps the best known of Britain''s unfinished hillforts: dumps of material from the abandoned works lie inside, and it has long been suspected no settlement stood there.', 'At 10.5 hectares, Ladle Hill''s fort was never finished. Unexcavated but sharply defined, its interrupted ditches reveal the step-by-step construction method Iron Age builders used.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c6bd2d66-f54d-4d02-8b14-041414043cf3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "At 10.5 hectares, Ladle Hill''s fort was never finished. Unexcavated but sharply defined, its interrupted ditches reveal the step-by-step construction method Iron Age builders used." restored on Ladle Hill', '45c5509a566b48ee416b2ff6a59e54672f856d605a0ae53cabc605bcc17999a7|fae8eee164155c50564d5ec6c361f03a0a841f4535d4bb2edd86fc9a8811f54d|fae8eee164155c50564d5ec6c361f03a0a841f4535d4bb2edd86fc9a8811f54d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ladle Hill is perhaps the best known of Britain''s unfinished hillforts: dumps of material from the abandoned works lie inside, and it has long been suspected no settlement stood there."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ladle Hill is perhaps the best known of Britain''s unfinished hillforts [S1]; dumps of material from the abandoned construction works lie in the interior [S4]; it has long been suspected that no settlement stood in the enclosed area [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ladle Hill is a hillfort (Iron Age, unfinished) in England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ladle_Hill]; Ladle Hill is an unfinished hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ladle_Hill]; It is perhaps the best known of Britain''s unfinished hillforts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ladle_Hill]; Dumps of material from the abandoned works lie inside [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ladle_Hill]; It has long been suspected that no settlement stood there [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ladle_Hill]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c858e9e8-6b70-45b6-9771-9f1e20c6dfa3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Its Welsh name means ''wooded slope of the ridge'': Allt yr Esgair rises to 393m above the Usk valley, an Iron Age hillfort on its summit and a Roman road running along its ridge.', 'Sitting atop a 393-metre ridge in Powys — Allt yr Esgair, "wooded slope of the ridge" — this hillfort commanded sightlines across the Usk valley and toward the Black Mountains.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c858e9e8-6b70-45b6-9771-9f1e20c6dfa3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Sitting atop a 393-metre ridge in Powys — Allt yr Esgair, "wooded slope of the ridge" — this hillfort commanded sightlines across the Usk valley and toward the Black Mountains.'' restored on Allt yr Esgair', '69e68d3bb17a52810de7ce53b227956fdb38cf8bea6481474db2a16da36d0d85|ae6b027c65e82a3f6c1006bda74dc87ca2da2eeed7eeaaaa059c4159fb0833b3|ae6b027c65e82a3f6c1006bda74dc87ca2da2eeed7eeaaaa059c4159fb0833b3', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Its Welsh name means ''wooded slope of the ridge'': Allt yr Esgair rises to 393m above the Usk valley, an Iron Age hillfort on its summit and a Roman road running along its ridge."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Welsh name Allt yr Esgair means ''wooded slope of the ridge'' [S3]; it rises to a height of 393m [S4]; it overlooks the valley of the River Usk [S4]; an Iron Age hillfort crowns its summit [S5]; a Roman road runs along its ridge [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Allt yr Esgair is a hill (with an Iron Age hillfort on its summit) in Wales above the Usk valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allt_yr_Esgair]; Its Welsh name means ''wooded slope of the ridge'' [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allt_yr_Esgair]; It rises to 393m above the Usk valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allt_yr_Esgair]; An Iron Age hillfort crowns the summit [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allt_yr_Esgair]; A Roman road runs along its ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allt_yr_Esgair]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c89e037a-1244-4d82-9dc4-4e97e18e6ad3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'By an important crossing over the Rhine, Argentovaria left oysters and fish from the Mediterranean and the remains of a bottle gourd from Africa, traces of trade that reached far.', 'Around the year 150 Ptolemy referred to the civil settlement of Argentovaria as the "second polis" of the Celtic Rauraci people after Augusta Raurica.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c89e037a-1244-4d82-9dc4-4e97e18e6ad3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Around the year 150 Ptolemy referred to the civil settlement of Argentovaria as the "second polis" of the Celtic Rauraci people after Augusta Raurica.'' restored on Argentovaria', '88689feeb6f8213e5c8d3b4f477adc49f119abc8f32c74890d2858bd1467c2e2|a028dad3b7b386b9a73242c3250e16ee35986a3d20468b56e4c2b4e9660de35b|a028dad3b7b386b9a73242c3250e16ee35986a3d20468b56e4c2b4e9660de35b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "By an important crossing over the Rhine, Argentovaria left oysters and fish from the Mediterranean and the remains of a bottle gourd from Africa, traces of trade that reached far."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Argentovaria lies at an important crossing over the Rhine [S1, S2]; finds at Argentovaria include oysters and fish from the Mediterranean [S6]; finds include the remains of a bottle gourd from Africa [S6]; these finds are traces of far-reaching trade [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Argentovaria is an ancient Roman military installation and civilian settlement at Biesheim in Alsace, France [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentovaria]; It lay by an important crossing over the Rhine [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentovaria]; Finds include oysters and fish from the Mediterranean [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentovaria]; Finds include the remains of a bottle gourd from Africa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentovaria]; These are traces of far-reaching trade [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentovaria]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c8a346a9-bed2-4420-83db-3a6fcca051e4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Scipio founded Italica in 206 BC for his Italic veterans. Here Hadrian, adopted son and successor of Trajan, built the Traianeum, a large, imposing temple in honour of the Emperor.', 'Founded in 206 BC by Scipio for Italian veterans, this city later produced two Roman emperors: Trajan and Hadrian. Its amphitheater seated 25,000.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c8a346a9-bed2-4420-83db-3a6fcca051e4:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Founded in 206 BC by Scipio for Italian veterans, this city later produced two Roman emperors: Trajan and Hadrian. Its amphitheater seated 25,000.'' restored on Italica', 'd8124e9b8364f717c73d52bbee8fa165a862d6f11093c2e009d2f70521508ded|3849f930126469fa435bd8791b7e470761dbb112af2881bb557679bceaf61ad3|3849f930126469fa435bd8791b7e470761dbb112af2881bb557679bceaf61ad3', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Scipio founded Italica in 206 BC for his Italic veterans. Here Hadrian, adopted son and successor of Trajan, built the Traianeum, a large, imposing temple in honour of the Emperor."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Scipio founded Italica [S2]; Italica was founded in 206 BC [S2]; it was founded for his Italic veterans [S2]; Hadrian was the adopted son and successor of Trajan [S6]; Hadrian built the Traianeum at Italica [S5, S6]; the Traianeum was a large, imposing temple in honour of the Emperor Trajan [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Italica is an ancient Roman city in Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italica]; Italica was founded in 206 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italica]; Scipio founded Italica [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italica]; Italica was founded for his Italic veterans [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italica]; Hadrian was adopted son and successor of Trajan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italica]; Hadrian built the Traianeum at Italica [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italica]; The Traianeum was a large, imposing temple in honour of the Emperor (Trajan) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italica]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c8d56e70-25e4-4814-b5c5-13cd14e68808'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Northeast of Barnstaple, Burridge Fort crowns a hilltop about 150 metres above sea level, an Iron Age hill fort looking out over the Yeo and Bradiford rivers below.', 'Hilltop fort at 150 metres above the Yeo and Bradiford rivers near Barnstaple in Devon. Only the outline of the earthwork defences survives above the surface today.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c8d56e70-25e4-4814-b5c5-13cd14e68808:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Hilltop fort at 150 metres above the Yeo and Bradiford rivers near Barnstaple in Devon. Only the outline of the earthwork defences survives above the surface today.'' restored on Burridge Fort', '921646e187254b19bd804e602f3f4f25e8a5a36ca6d64d9b783996940cd2dab0|cee96f2c2340ab05e242abde8ac514f035f92df7188f89c36166ceb66fc890de|cee96f2c2340ab05e242abde8ac514f035f92df7188f89c36166ceb66fc890de', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Northeast of Barnstaple, Burridge Fort crowns a hilltop about 150 metres above sea level, an Iron Age hill fort looking out over the Yeo and Bradiford rivers below."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Burridge Fort lies northeast of Barnstaple [S1]; it occupies a hilltop about 150 metres above sea level [S2]; it is an Iron Age hill fort [S1]; it overlooks the Yeo and Bradiford rivers [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Burridge Fort is a hill fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burridge_Fort]; It dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burridge_Fort]; It lies northeast of Barnstaple (Devon, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burridge_Fort]; It crowns a hilltop about 150 metres above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burridge_Fort]; It overlooks the Yeo and Bradiford rivers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burridge_Fort]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c961d960-661c-4406-a645-4185684b4c89'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the southwest coast of Gozo, the promontory of Ras il-Wardija holds a Punic-Roman sanctuary. Carved crosses on its walls suggest it later became a Christian place of worship.', 'Ruins of a Punic-Roman sanctuary on Gozo''s southwest coast, dating from 1500 BC to the 4th century AD. The promontory site was used for millennia.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:c961d960-661c-4406-a645-4185684b4c89:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "Ruins of a Punic-Roman sanctuary on Gozo''s southwest coast, dating from 1500 BC to the 4th century AD. The promontory site was used for millennia." restored on Ras il-Wardija', '2f78bf4ebb9f5284171b7b5011de69fa8f745009e4c98a4e47e25b2d74bd37b8|685b3a6049dffe1cf1f9d546c49bcb87419073e233845541d4eba9bafc263c4f|685b3a6049dffe1cf1f9d546c49bcb87419073e233845541d4eba9bafc263c4f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the southwest coast of Gozo, the promontory of Ras il-Wardija holds a Punic-Roman sanctuary. Carved crosses on its walls suggest it later became a Christian place of worship."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ras il-Wardija is on the southwest coast of Gozo [S1]; Ras il-Wardija is a promontory [S1]; it holds the remains of a Punic-Roman sanctuary [S2]; there are carved crosses on its walls [S6]; the crosses suggest the site later became a Christian place of worship [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ras il-Wardija holds a sanctuary (the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ras_il-Wardija]; Ras il-Wardija is a promontory [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ras_il-Wardija]; It lies on the southwest coast of Gozo (Malta) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ras_il-Wardija]; The sanctuary is Punic-Roman [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ras_il-Wardija]; Carved crosses are on its walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ras_il-Wardija]; The crosses suggest the site later became a Christian place of worship [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ras_il-Wardija]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cab41f26-0bf2-4078-a0c9-6e173fcdc837'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Six columns and a sacred well in the courtyard mark Barran Temple. Inscriptions on its walls describe events of the Sabaean state, so it may have served as a documentation center.', 'A 10th-century BC Sabaean temple dedicated to the moon god Al-Maqah. Six columns and a sacred well survive from this ancient Arabian kingdom.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cab41f26-0bf2-4078-a0c9-6e173fcdc837:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A 10th-century BC Sabaean temple dedicated to the moon god Al-Maqah. Six columns and a sacred well survive from this ancient Arabian kingdom.'' restored on Throne of Queen Sheba - Barran Temple', '28d3bab8e27fbee85f376ad3050092161aa797cb0d56a46b98ea1eed5ba9d312|7c5e97586f2681f9efd856147193fea715578f58272faabeb58406ca4351caef|7c5e97586f2681f9efd856147193fea715578f58272faabeb58406ca4351caef', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Six columns and a sacred well in the courtyard mark Barran Temple. Inscriptions on its walls describe events of the Sabaean state, so it may have served as a documentation center."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the temple has six columns [S4]; it has a sacred well in the courtyard [S4]; inscriptions were found on its walls [S8]; the inscriptions describe events surrounding the Sabaean state [S8]; it may have served as a documentation center (hedged) [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-006 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Barran Temple is a Sabaean temple near Marib, Yemen (the Throne of Bilqis / Queen Sheba) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bar%27an_Temple]; The temple has six columns in its courtyard [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bar%27an_Temple]; The temple has a sacred well in the courtyard [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bar%27an_Temple]; Inscriptions on the walls describe events of the Sabaean state [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bar%27an_Temple]; It may have served as a documentation center [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bar%27an_Temple]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cac857fa-bbb6-4364-a0f3-64bb9b9a7e4b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The last of the great ancient cities of Taxila, Sirsukh was ringed by a wall about 5.4 meters thick, its circular bastions pierced with holes for archers to shoot at the enemy.', 'The last of Taxila''s great ancient cities, founded around 80 AD during the Kushan era. Each successive ruler abandoned the old city and built a new one.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cac857fa-bbb6-4364-a0f3-64bb9b9a7e4b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "The last of Taxila''s great ancient cities, founded around 80 AD during the Kushan era. Each successive ruler abandoned the old city and built a new one." restored on Sirsukh', '3c614272614a715037156bb840862f8ccb258759988d576b3d708739235d8f01|dbd2f2b14b3bbcf568f9d1a12a34bcd4ba9fcc16b5850ad5f411f2d8cc3c3a4c|dbd2f2b14b3bbcf568f9d1a12a34bcd4ba9fcc16b5850ad5f411f2d8cc3c3a4c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The last of the great ancient cities of Taxila, Sirsukh was ringed by a wall about 5.4 meters thick, its circular bastions pierced with holes for archers to shoot at the enemy."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sirsukh is the last of the great ancient cities of Taxila [S2]; the city had a wall about 5.4 meters thick [S3]; the wall had circular bastions [S6]; the bastions had holes for archers to shoot at the enemy [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sirsukh is an ancient city of Taxila (the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirsukh]; It is the last of the great ancient cities of Taxila [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirsukh]; It was ringed by a city wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirsukh]; The wall was about 5.4 meters thick [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirsukh]; The wall had circular bastions [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirsukh]; The bastions were pierced with holes for archers to shoot at the enemy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sirsukh]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cb3fa612-c066-4646-ba63-ec80fa8c57c2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Fourvière heights, west of where the Rhône meets the Saône, Lugdunum was the birthplace of Claudius and Caracalla, and from 274 AD the sole mint of the western empire.', 'Founded in 43 BC as a Roman colony, Lugdunum served as capital of all three Gallic provinces. Two Roman emperors — Claudius and Caracalla — were born here.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cb3fa612-c066-4646-ba63-ec80fa8c57c2:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Founded in 43 BC as a Roman colony, Lugdunum served as capital of all three Gallic provinces. Two Roman emperors — Claudius and Caracalla — were born here.'' restored on Lugdunum', 'ed5660ca81e3487e0dc767e96f227841cb9be4f94c1484a65fc7cd6499b0581b|499dc9cdd86a767d8a6ad486aa4dcf572d040029b3e86c852a388e63486a7ad4|499dc9cdd86a767d8a6ad486aa4dcf572d040029b3e86c852a388e63486a7ad4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Fourvière heights, west of where the Rhône meets the Saône, Lugdunum was the birthplace of Claudius and Caracalla, and from 274 AD the sole mint of the western empire."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Lugdunum was situated on the Fourvière heights [S5]; it lay west of the confluence of the Rhône and the Saône [S5]; Claudius was born in Lugdunum [S4]; Caracalla was born in Lugdunum [S4]; from 274 AD Lugdunum was the sole mint for the western empire [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Lugdunum was a Roman city (the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lugdunum]; It stood on the Fourviere heights [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lugdunum]; It lay west of the confluence of the Rhone and the Saone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lugdunum]; Claudius was born in Lugdunum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lugdunum]; Caracalla was born in Lugdunum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lugdunum]; From 274 AD Lugdunum was the sole mint of the western empire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lugdunum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cbca1610-17fd-49f8-9c3e-536db4f7c9ce'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Under the Ostrusha Mound, a Thracian tomb near Shipka, a chamber of two solid granite blocks keeps its painted roof. Most frescoes are damaged, yet a young noble woman''s portrait survives.', 'A 4th-century BC Thracian burial mound over 18 metres high, with six chambers covering 100 square metres. One chamber is carved from two solid granite blocks totaling 60 tonnes.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cbca1610-17fd-49f8-9c3e-536db4f7c9ce:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A 4th-century BC Thracian burial mound over 18 metres high, with six chambers covering 100 square metres. One chamber is carved from two solid granite blocks totaling 60 tonnes.'' restored on Ostrusha Mound', '20845f7b41c09324078381ad027374532dc911d9e1776a65802c5acad9f137f5|744191f28a2850dca4b1b0da4bf4bb2924c040a66ba4ea61f52f005c6c299bf1|744191f28a2850dca4b1b0da4bf4bb2924c040a66ba4ea61f52f005c6c299bf1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Under the Ostrusha Mound, a Thracian tomb near Shipka, a chamber of two solid granite blocks keeps its painted roof. Most frescoes are damaged, yet a young noble woman''s portrait survives."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Ostrusha Mound is a Thracian tomb (burial tumulus) [S1]; it lies near Shipka [S1]; a chamber lies under the mound [S3, S4]; the chamber is made of two solid granite blocks [S5]; the chamber still keeps its roof [S4]; the roof is painted [S6]; most of the frescoes are damaged [S7]; a portrait of a young noble woman survives [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ostrusha Mound is a Thracian tomb (burial tumulus) (the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrusha_mound]; It is near Shipka (Bulgaria) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrusha_mound]; The tomb structures lie under the mound [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrusha_mound]; A chamber is made of two solid granite blocks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrusha_mound]; The chamber keeps its painted roof [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrusha_mound]; Most of the frescoes are damaged [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrusha_mound]; A portrait of a young noble woman survives [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrusha_mound]; The painted chamber is fully maintained (the roof survives) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostrusha_mound]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cbfd3d2a-70f5-41f4-a3db-298b56040fea'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On Dere Street stood Vindomora, a Roman auxiliary fort at Ebchester. Its stones name the Centurions who built it, and an early 3rd century altar names a garrison levied from the Breuci.', 'An auxiliary fort on Dere Street in Lower Britain, positioned between Corbridge and Bywell to control a key supply corridor. Its ruins — Ebchester Roman Fort — sit in County Durham north of Consett.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cbfd3d2a-70f5-41f4-a3db-298b56040fea:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''An auxiliary fort on Dere Street in Lower Britain, positioned between Corbridge and Bywell to control a key supply corridor. Its ruins — Ebchester Roman Fort — sit in County Durham north of Consett.'' restored on Vindomora', '1c5803d7897827a779fb02e870c5d2e83aca4122b34208beaede7434d672d9f4|707c77ebba8e6e7960dd6ddf3ddb5f20366140cfdb8dc8b73e8647d4503e745e|707c77ebba8e6e7960dd6ddf3ddb5f20366140cfdb8dc8b73e8647d4503e745e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Dere Street stood Vindomora, a Roman auxiliary fort at Ebchester. Its stones name the Centurions who built it, and an early 3rd century altar names a garrison levied from the Breuci."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Vindomora stood on Dere Street [S1]; it was a Roman auxiliary fort [S1, S2]; it is at Ebchester [S2]; stones from the fort name the Centurions responsible for building it [S5]; an early 3rd century altar identifies a garrison unit [S6]; that garrison unit was levied from the Breuci [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Vindomora was a Roman auxiliary fort (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindomora]; It stood on Dere Street [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindomora]; The fort is at Ebchester [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindomora]; Stones name the Centurions who built the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindomora]; An early 3rd century altar names the garrison, the Fourth Cohort of the Breuci [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindomora]; The garrison unit was levied from the Breuci [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindomora]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cc7e7e8a-ad91-4001-9849-bfcd41168369'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a Northumberland hillside, the Slate Hill Settlement shields the north and west of its Iron Age enclosure with four concentric ramparts of stone and earth; a steep slope guards the south.', 'A defended Iron Age settlement first built between the 7th and 5th centuries BC. Enclosures like these housed several round timber buildings inside.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cc7e7e8a-ad91-4001-9849-bfcd41168369:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A defended Iron Age settlement first built between the 7th and 5th centuries BC. Enclosures like these housed several round timber buildings inside.'' restored on Slate Hill Settlement', 'a20716191dee846bc59a7d41a82d6a8c18e1f253871dc9be44836007e9d280ff|3e13dba5e65339ad64d6be8e13e348241226c8bd763b586ba96fa2e8cfb62b96|3e13dba5e65339ad64d6be8e13e348241226c8bd763b586ba96fa2e8cfb62b96', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a Northumberland hillside, the Slate Hill Settlement shields the north and west of its Iron Age enclosure with four concentric ramparts of stone and earth; a steep slope guards the south."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Slate Hill Settlement is in Northumberland [S1]; it lies on a sloping hillside [S4]; it has an Iron Age enclosure [S2, S4]; four concentric ramparts shield the north and west sides of the enclosure [S4]; the ramparts are of stone and earth [S5]; a steep slope defends the south side [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Slate Hill Settlement is a defended Iron Age settlement in Northumberland (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slate_Hill_Settlement]; It is in Northumberland, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slate_Hill_Settlement]; Four concentric ramparts shield the north and west sides of the enclosure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slate_Hill_Settlement]; The ramparts are of stone and earth [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slate_Hill_Settlement]; The enclosure is Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slate_Hill_Settlement]; The ramparts lie on the sloping hillside [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slate_Hill_Settlement]; A steep slope guards the south [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slate_Hill_Settlement]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cc8c8638-cb77-4b61-a248-5c0f8e19cb66'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Dur-Sharrukin, the Assyrian capital of Sargon II, was built in the decade before 706 BC: massive walls guarded by 157 towers, seven gates, and a walled terrace of temples and a royal palace.', 'An Assyrian capital with 24-metre-thick city walls, abandoned after King Sargon II died in battle. The entire city was built from scratch and deserted within a generation.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cc8c8638-cb77-4b61-a248-5c0f8e19cb66:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''An Assyrian capital with 24-metre-thick city walls, abandoned after King Sargon II died in battle. The entire city was built from scratch and deserted within a generation.'' restored on Dur-Sharrukin', 'f2d7148417a2633b5d7c1c0f5daf8a800a4de9822119750a5cbde3c30a75de70|a9858c51cc4c6b3c8c030876583485e1f9c07d9fc361566a975acaeff4719614|a9858c51cc4c6b3c8c030876583485e1f9c07d9fc361566a975acaeff4719614', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dur-Sharrukin, the Assyrian capital of Sargon II, was built in the decade before 706 BC: massive walls guarded by 157 towers, seven gates, and a walled terrace of temples and a royal palace."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dur-Sharrukin was the Assyrian capital [S1]; it was the capital in the time of Sargon II [S1]; it was built in the decade before 706 BC [S2]; it had massive walls [S5]; 157 towers protected the walls [S5]; it had seven gates [S6]; a walled terrace held temples and a royal palace [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dur-Sharrukin was the Assyrian capital of Sargon II (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dur-Sharrukin]; It is in Iraq (Khorsabad, northern Iraq) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dur-Sharrukin]; It was built in the decade before 706 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dur-Sharrukin]; Massive walls guarded by 157 towers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dur-Sharrukin]; The city walls were massive [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dur-Sharrukin]; Seven gates entered the city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dur-Sharrukin]; A walled terrace contained temples and the royal palace [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dur-Sharrukin]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ccb3de8b-5c50-4204-ae6f-46cdda2e081d'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'A settlement with 4,000 years of continuous use, famous for its vivid red-painted pottery. Bronze tools found here rewrote the timeline of Southeast Asian metallurgy.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:ccb3de8b-5c50-4204-ae6f-46cdda2e081d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A settlement with 4,000 years of continuous use, famous for its vivid red-painted pottery. Bronze tools found here rewrote the timeline of Southeast Asian metallurgy.'' restored on Ban Chiang', '||214f2a5947ee3a3ce57b90240df3f2f0c270edde05d7b6a3d9b09e531c80fc4e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: unproven-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check could not prove: Ban Chiang is an archaeological site in Thailand (central claim); The web check could not prove: Ban Chiang is an archaeological site in Udon Thani province, Thailand"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ccf25f6a-36aa-458a-9d43-5b33ef8c119c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'East of Daventry, Borough Hill rises over 200 metres above sea level, with two Bronze Age barrows, two Iron Age hillforts, one the fourth largest found in Britain, and a later Roman villa.', 'A hill with Bronze Age barrows, two Iron Age hillforts -- one the fourth largest in all of Britain -- and a Roman villa. Four thousand years of occupation on one summit.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:ccf25f6a-36aa-458a-9d43-5b33ef8c119c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A hill with Bronze Age barrows, two Iron Age hillforts -- one the fourth largest in all of Britain -- and a Roman villa. Four thousand years of occupation on one summit.'' restored on Borough Hill', '7c7b3b70f589281c98425ba49338b8fda0637ab5a5447e1f25ba63986929f61a|19e04720d708404cc7a2ea4450fb5306c9ba5434598e3bdd3a1b73b41f1968ce|19e04720d708404cc7a2ea4450fb5306c9ba5434598e3bdd3a1b73b41f1968ce', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "East of Daventry, Borough Hill rises over 200 metres above sea level, with two Bronze Age barrows, two Iron Age hillforts, one the fourth largest found in Britain, and a later Roman villa."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Borough Hill is east of Daventry [S1]; it rises over 200 metres above sea level [S2]; it has two Bronze Age barrows [S4]; it has two Iron Age hillforts [S4]; one hillfort is the fourth largest found in Britain [S4]; it has a later Roman villa [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Borough Hill is a hill with prehistoric and Roman remains (central claim: a hill east of Daventry) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borough_Hill]; It lies east of Daventry [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borough_Hill]; It rises over 200 metres above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borough_Hill]; Two Bronze Age barrows [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borough_Hill]; Two Iron Age hillforts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borough_Hill]; One hillfort is the fourth largest found in Britain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borough_Hill]; A later Roman villa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borough_Hill]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cd307f24-5170-4cb1-acb0-0e7ba7e276d7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Begun in AD 142, the Antonine Wall was once the northernmost frontier of the Roman Empire: turf on stone foundations, a deep ditch to the north, and 16 forts between the Clyde and the Forth.', 'The Antonine Wall was protected by 16 forts with small fortlets between them; troop movement was facilitated by a road linking all the sites known as the Military Way.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cd307f24-5170-4cb1-acb0-0e7ba7e276d7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''The Antonine Wall was protected by 16 forts with small fortlets between them; troop movement was facilitated by a road linking all the sites known as the Military Way.'' restored on Antonine Wall', '1dce619a87d46d36affccf540a9a7954a03b1dc0efada6dd5deb9686bc9b8403|4b988bd84303d9235a9348108f31870a9227e7ce848a51154835f80d265b7cc3|4b988bd84303d9235a9348108f31870a9227e7ce848a51154835f80d265b7cc3', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Begun in AD 142, the Antonine Wall was once the northernmost frontier of the Roman Empire: turf on stone foundations, a deep ditch to the north, and 16 forts between the Clyde and the Forth."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Antonine Wall was begun in AD 142 [S6]; it was once the northernmost frontier of the Roman Empire [S2]; it was turf on stone foundations [S1]; a deep ditch lay on its northern side [S4]; it had 16 forts [S7]; it ran between the Clyde and the Forth [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Antonine Wall was a Roman frontier fortification across Scotland, once the northernmost frontier of the Roman Empire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonine_Wall]; Construction began in AD 142 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonine_Wall]; The wall was turf on stone foundations [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonine_Wall]; A deep ditch lay on the north side [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonine_Wall]; It had 16 forts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonine_Wall]; The wall ran between the Clyde and the Forth [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonine_Wall]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ce101d86-8313-4fe1-bb90-9c74fba2a76e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Sanctuary of Asclepius at Epidaurus rivalled the major cult sites of Olympia and Delphi. In 146 BC the Achaians made it a stronghold; in 87 BC the Roman general Sulla looted it.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:ce101d86-8313-4fe1-bb90-9c74fba2a76e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description None restored on Sanctuary of Asclepius, Epidaurus', '50119fcd0b01aabad9c937b3d4e3b36fe9d1c34fd0bbdb31a1a07be8b3d04ce9|5e70654512b7c4ef855ddcc9706f74242372bd1268957c03585bf9a738753d9e|5e70654512b7c4ef855ddcc9706f74242372bd1268957c03585bf9a738753d9e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Sanctuary of Asclepius at Epidaurus rivalled the major cult sites of Olympia and Delphi. In 146 BC the Achaians made it a stronghold; in 87 BC the Roman general Sulla looted it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Sanctuary of Asclepius is at Epidaurus [S1]; it rivalled the major cult sites of Olympia and Delphi [S3]; in 146 BC the Achaians converted it into a stronghold [S4]; in 87 BC the Roman general Sulla looted it [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Sanctuary of Asclepius at Epidaurus is a sanctuary dedicated to Asclepius in Epidaurus, Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Asclepius,_Epidaurus]; It rivalled the major cult sites of Olympia and Delphi [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Asclepius,_Epidaurus]; In 146 BC the Achaians made it a stronghold [SUPPORTED: https://en.wik