    ('ce1d8e3d-924e-4667-9ba6-9e32a3fac6d3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Koviljkin grad crowns a hilltop: ruins of a Roman town that may have been named Gensis, a name never confirmed. The Drina once flowed below, where a river harbour is believed to have been.', 'Occupying a hilltop in Loznica district, this Roman town''s ancient name — Gensis — has never been confirmed. Below the walls, a harbour operated on the river Drina.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:ce1d8e3d-924e-4667-9ba6-9e32a3fac6d3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "Occupying a hilltop in Loznica district, this Roman town''s ancient name — Gensis — has never been confirmed. Below the walls, a harbour operated on the river Drina." restored on Koviljkin grad', '3e9f68781d642f51364ca73a6dbb818891736925f2155ca0c7b90703bd8688a2|8d25f676ec75cf3afe2ae1fab8fe6ecb9bd5a78429c6bd3b5d27d81b1a320d46|8d25f676ec75cf3afe2ae1fab8fe6ecb9bd5a78429c6bd3b5d27d81b1a320d46', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Koviljkin grad crowns a hilltop: ruins of a Roman town that may have been named Gensis, a name never confirmed. The Drina once flowed below, where a river harbour is believed to have been."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Koviljkin grad is on a hilltop [S3]; the ruins are of a Roman town [S2]; the town may have been named Gensis [S2]; the name Gensis has never been confirmed (an unknown) [S2]; the Drina once flowed below the settlement [S4]; a river harbour is believed to have been there (hedged) [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Koviljkin grad is an archaeological site near Banja Koviljaca in western Serbia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koviljkin_grad]; Koviljkin grad is the archaeological ruins of a Roman town near Banja Koviljaca, Serbia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koviljkin_grad]; It sits on top of a hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koviljkin_grad]; The town may have been named Gensis, a name never confirmed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koviljkin_grad]; The Drina once flowed below the settlement [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koviljkin_grad]; A river harbour is believed to have been there [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koviljkin_grad]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ce979635-b9c3-4645-838d-a7a30da06854'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a steep knoll near Shetland''s cliffs and sea, the unexcavated Broch of Culswick keeps a massive triangular lintel stone over its entrance, the passage behind it nearly filled with debris.', 'An unexcavated coastal broch with views to Foula, Vaila, and Fair Isle. A massive triangular lintel stone still sits over the entrance despite centuries of collapse.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:ce979635-b9c3-4645-838d-a7a30da06854:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''An unexcavated coastal broch with views to Foula, Vaila, and Fair Isle. A massive triangular lintel stone still sits over the entrance despite centuries of collapse.'' restored on Broch of Culswick', '7a506ebd557151d14d15c3d0cb0aafb1e2389c4703389172482f77870d17bc58|1a1d4b8f56bec17b84cb2f81bd078754843187dcbd0a6982c3dced34592548a2|1a1d4b8f56bec17b84cb2f81bd078754843187dcbd0a6982c3dced34592548a2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a steep knoll near Shetland''s cliffs and sea, the unexcavated Broch of Culswick keeps a massive triangular lintel stone over its entrance, the passage behind it nearly filled with debris."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Broch of Culswick is on a steep knoll [S6]; it is near cliffs and the sea [S6]; it is in Shetland [S1]; it is unexcavated [S1]; a massive triangular lintel stone is over its entrance [S3]; the entrance passage is nearly filled with debris [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The lintel over the entrance is a massive triangular stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Culswick]; The Broch of Culswick is a coastal broch in the Shetland Islands of Scotland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Culswick]; It stands on a steep knoll near cliffs and the sea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Culswick]; It is unexcavated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Culswick]; It has a massive triangular lintel stone over the entrance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Culswick]; The passage behind the entrance is nearly filled with debris [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Culswick]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ced822e8-13cd-49db-9011-808dc23aac45'::uuid, 'card_description', 'By a small inlet in Attica, Brauron is celebrated for the worship of Artemis. Its spring gave up many childish playthings, tokens a young girl dedicated to Artemis on the eve of marriage.', 'An ancient city with a sanctuary of Artemis Brauronia, protector of women in childbirth. Young girls performed a bear-dance ritual here before they could marry.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:ced822e8-13cd-49db-9011-808dc23aac45:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''An ancient city with a sanctuary of Artemis Brauronia, protector of women in childbirth. Young girls performed a bear-dance ritual here before they could marry.'' restored on Brauron', '51690ce8cba4ac9fa25d8e30e3212560f118d06a565000a8fb8a741d3daa00fb|b8b2cc0335015e009cef4e085e32910f2fa9636541951dbb7c940c57552b6f85|b8b2cc0335015e009cef4e085e32910f2fa9636541951dbb7c940c57552b6f85', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "By a small inlet in Attica, Brauron is celebrated for the worship of Artemis. Its spring gave up many childish playthings, tokens a young girl dedicated to Artemis on the eve of marriage."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Brauron is in Attica [S1, S3]; it lies by a small inlet [S3]; Brauron is celebrated for the worship of Artemis [S2]; many childish playthings were recovered from its spring [S7]; these were tokens of the kind a young girl dedicated to Artemis on the eve of marriage [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Brauron is a site on the Attic coast of Greece, a sanctuary of Artemis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brauron]; It lies by a small inlet in Attica [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brauron]; Brauron is celebrated for the worship of Artemis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brauron]; Many childish playthings dedicated to Artemis were recovered from its spring [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brauron]; These were tokens a young girl dedicated to Artemis on the eve of marriage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brauron]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cee33cc0-9de5-493c-b43b-82678a6b1d8b'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'A city known since Roman times for its hot springs, called the Burgas. It remains one of the European cities with the greatest thermal heritage.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cee33cc0-9de5-493c-b43b-82678a6b1d8b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A city known since Roman times for its hot springs, called the Burgas. It remains one of the European cities with the greatest thermal heritage.'' restored on Ourense', '||5fb860eaf6396196c06c49491e116c32a888686fdb20f2db49247cab36b7f294', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: unproven-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check could not prove: Ourense is a city (ancient city of Auria) located in Galicia, Spain; The web check could not prove: Ourense/Auria spans both banks of the Miño river; The web check could not prove: Auria is the ancient name of the city of Ourense; The web check could not prove: Ourense is a city in Galicia, Spain (an urban settlement of Roman origin)"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cf7d3acb-1b74-484d-8084-6c7297b23ec0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Deep in the Caves of Arcy-sur-Cure, 300 to 500 m from the entrance, the oldest wall paintings are 28,000 years old, the second oldest parietal art presently known after the Chauvet Cave.', 'Dug by the Cure river in a limestone massif from the Mesozoic, the caves were used as shelter by humans since at least 200,000 years ago.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cf7d3acb-1b74-484d-8084-6c7297b23ec0:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Dug by the Cure river in a limestone massif from the Mesozoic, the caves were used as shelter by humans since at least 200,000 years ago.'' restored on Caves of Arcy-sur-Cure', '3e69a8d7ea7fc2370fc9a0690c691f4d8a061f224b00bf911fcf14c02e7e76fe|c5ae2f05b1213198ccbe857fc648e755ff975b273a4300946d84d3e3f91da6f1|c5ae2f05b1213198ccbe857fc648e755ff975b273a4300946d84d3e3f91da6f1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Deep in the Caves of Arcy-sur-Cure, 300 to 500 m from the entrance, the oldest wall paintings are 28,000 years old, the second oldest parietal art presently known after the Chauvet Cave."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the wall paintings in the Caves of Arcy-sur-Cure lie deep inside, 300 to 500 m from the entrance [S6]; the oldest of the paintings are 28,000 years old [S6]; the caves hold the second oldest parietal art presently known [S3]; only the Chauvet Cave''s parietal art is older [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cornalvo Dam is a Roman gravity dam [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornalvo_Dam]; Built in the 1st-2nd century AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornalvo_Dam]; Built to supply water to Emerita Augusta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornalvo_Dam]; Emerita Augusta was capital of Lusitania [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornalvo_Dam]; The dam belongs to the Archaeological Ensemble of Mérida [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornalvo_Dam]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cf82ed54-d7e1-4a86-9b24-2abfdb6ff881'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Cornalvo Dam, a Roman gravity dam of the 1st–2nd century AD, was built to supply water to Emerita Augusta, capital of Lusitania, and belongs to the Archaeological Ensemble of Mérida.', 'The Cornalvo Dam is a Roman gravity dam built to supply water to the Roman colonia of Emerita Augusta, capital of the Roman province of Lusitania.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cf82ed54-d7e1-4a86-9b24-2abfdb6ff881:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''The Cornalvo Dam is a Roman gravity dam built to supply water to the Roman colonia of Emerita Augusta, capital of the Roman province of Lusitania.'' restored on Cornalvo Dam', 'a1d2045cdac0da503ea2da71665bfe10defaa9e685bb4c993be7856d2f442a4e|8d6b2cf5d43d80ce2d1f65ba4f54162730e8aa2282b608f6626ad8bffb3dfd6a|8d6b2cf5d43d80ce2d1f65ba4f54162730e8aa2282b608f6626ad8bffb3dfd6a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Cornalvo Dam, a Roman gravity dam of the 1st–2nd century AD, was built to supply water to Emerita Augusta, capital of Lusitania, and belongs to the Archaeological Ensemble of Mérida."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Cornalvo Dam is a Roman gravity dam [S1]; it dates from the 1st-2nd century AD [S2]; it was built to supply water to Emerita Augusta [S1]; Emerita Augusta was the capital of Lusitania [S1]; the dam belongs to the Archaeological Ensemble of Mérida [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Caves of Arcy-sur-Cure are a series of caves with wall paintings, in France [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caves_of_Arcy-sur-Cure]; The paintings are in cave rooms 300 to 500 m from the entrance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caves_of_Arcy-sur-Cure]; The oldest wall paintings are 28,000 years old [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caves_of_Arcy-sur-Cure]; They are the second oldest parietal art presently known after the Chauvet Cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caves_of_Arcy-sur-Cure]; The oldest wall paintings are in the Great cave of the complex (parietal art) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caves_of_Arcy-sur-Cure]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cfd35d85-208d-4cab-ade6-118647969036'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Ludgate was the westernmost gate in London Wall, believed to be one of its four original Roman gates. A legend recorded by Geoffrey of Monmouth names it after the ancient British King Lud.', 'The westernmost gate of London''s Roman wall, rebuilt several times before its demolition in 1760. Its name survives in Ludgate Hill, the street leading to St. Paul''s Cathedral.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cfd35d85-208d-4cab-ade6-118647969036:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "The westernmost gate of London''s Roman wall, rebuilt several times before its demolition in 1760. Its name survives in Ludgate Hill, the street leading to St. Paul''s Cathedral." restored on Ludgate', 'aa34c872d6e931dc862cbe277ce5360615b7dc2dc969c352c908fe5ad32af06c|19e50800780cb24a342fcb053f746a62e180d516f2bbc542c38acc3681503810|19e50800780cb24a342fcb053f746a62e180d516f2bbc542c38acc3681503810', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ludgate was the westernmost gate in London Wall, believed to be one of its four original Roman gates. A legend recorded by Geoffrey of Monmouth names it after the ancient British King Lud."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ludgate was the westernmost gate in London Wall [S1]; it is believed to be one of the four original gates of the Roman London Wall (hedged) [S3]; a legend recorded by Geoffrey of Monmouth says it was named after King Lud [S2]; King Lud was an ancient British king [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ludgate was a gate in London Wall (a city gate in London, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludgate]; Ludgate was the westernmost gate in London Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludgate]; Believed to be one of its four original Roman gates [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludgate]; A legend recorded by Geoffrey of Monmouth names it after the ancient British King Lud [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludgate]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cfffa587-4250-43cb-89c2-84cad4e21c8a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Beneath Corndon Hill lies Whetstones Stone Circle, its shape unrecorded. In 1841 only three stones remained, all leaning: mutilated fragments of the ring it once was.', 'Neolithic stone circle beneath Corndon Hill, 4th millennium BC. Near the Hoarstones and Mitchell''s Fold circles, the Whetstones formed a cluster of ritual monuments on the border with Shropshire.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:cfffa587-4250-43cb-89c2-84cad4e21c8a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "Neolithic stone circle beneath Corndon Hill, 4th millennium BC. Near the Hoarstones and Mitchell''s Fold circles, the Whetstones formed a cluster of ritual monuments on the border with Shropshire." restored on Whetstones Stone Circle', '027f8808ff1ce830b27089a72717feeb9ed4731e0a2bc77ebecde8ace17e044b|0e41c7c60bde8a17a8230cc0ecc550fd0eaefcd389466646a628620b5706881a|0e41c7c60bde8a17a8230cc0ecc550fd0eaefcd389466646a628620b5706881a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Beneath Corndon Hill lies Whetstones Stone Circle, its shape unrecorded. In 1841 only three stones remained, all leaning: mutilated fragments of the ring it once was."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Whetstones Stone Circle is a stone circle [S1]; it lies beneath Corndon Hill [S1, S2]; its shape is unrecorded [S4]; in 1841 only three stones remained [S5]; all three stones were leaning [S5]; the stones were mutilated fragments of the ring''s original form [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Whetstones is a stone circle beneath Corndon Hill (Wales) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whetstones_(stone_circle)]; The shape of the circle is unrecorded [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whetstones_(stone_circle)]; In 1841 only three stones remained [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whetstones_(stone_circle)]; The three stones were all leaning [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whetstones_(stone_circle)]; The stones were mutilated fragments of the original ring [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whetstones_(stone_circle)]; The remaining stones are what is left of a once complete ring that was destroyed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whetstones_(stone_circle)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d0f8e6a4-9c45-4e1a-a4d6-c76efaacc88c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Pont Serme, the Pons Selinus, later the Pons Septimus, carried the Via Domitia at Coursan in the Aude: a Roman bridge that surpassed even Trajan''s Bridge over the Danube in length.', '1st century AD Roman viaduct c. 1,500 m long, crossing the Aude marshes -- longer than Trajan''s bridge over the Danube. Today almost nothing remains except its name, preserved by a nearby wine estate.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d0f8e6a4-9c45-4e1a-a4d6-c76efaacc88c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "1st century AD Roman viaduct c. 1,500 m long, crossing the Aude marshes -- longer than Trajan''s bridge over the Danube. Today almost nothing remains except its name, preserved by a nearby wine estate." restored on Pont Serme', '32af075e5fe17405bc6b6b717f6839961f3012ba64650f14a313ceeb21264ccf|46e94f32e00ee0e2093203274cfe2e46341c84eeed52b605d215e1a4bbf09fb7|46e94f32e00ee0e2093203274cfe2e46341c84eeed52b605d215e1a4bbf09fb7', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pont Serme, the Pons Selinus, later the Pons Septimus, carried the Via Domitia at Coursan in the Aude: a Roman bridge that surpassed even Trajan''s Bridge over the Danube in length."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-003 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pont Serme was also called the Pons Selinus [S1]; it was later called the Pons Septimus [S1]; it carried the Via Domitia [S1]; it was at Coursan in the Aude [S1]; it was a Roman bridge [S1]; it surpassed Trajan''s Bridge over the Danube in length [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-007 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-20