    ('d138312f-d127-427e-beac-f0210cf41ba8'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Thick woods in Somerset cloak Wraxall Camp, an Iron Age settlement whose homes sat within a bank and ditch. Digs in 1928 found black burnished pottery and a Kimmeridge shale bracelet.', 'A small round earthwork that looks defensive but was actually an Iron Age farmstead. Dense woodland now obscures its modest banks and ditches.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d138312f-d127-427e-beac-f0210cf41ba8:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A small round earthwork that looks defensive but was actually an Iron Age farmstead. Dense woodland now obscures its modest banks and ditches.'' restored on Wraxall Camp', 'f6ddb6a67e8e45153fe028ff11b4362af8d53e6e728e8d9225bd9b280fc9c4da|a7810cbaad34f014acc7ca49823f7a3e1c1df7ca72f7f002e6b791d43aa18eb5|a7810cbaad34f014acc7ca49823f7a3e1c1df7ca72f7f002e6b791d43aa18eb5', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Thick woods in Somerset cloak Wraxall Camp, an Iron Age settlement whose homes sat within a bank and ditch. Digs in 1928 found black burnished pottery and a Kimmeridge shale bracelet."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wraxall Camp is thickly covered by woods [S2]; it is in Somerset [S1, S3]; it is an Iron Age settlement [S5]; its homes lay within a bank and ditch [S7]; excavations in 1928 found black burnished pottery [S6]; the 1928 excavations found a Kimmeridge shale bracelet [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wraxall Camp is an Iron Age settlement (a small round earthwork) in Somerset [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wraxall_Camp]; The site lies in Somerset [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wraxall_Camp]; The site is thickly covered by woods [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wraxall_Camp]; The homes sat within a bank and ditch [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wraxall_Camp]; Excavations in 1928 found black burnished pottery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wraxall_Camp]; Excavations in 1928 found a Kimmeridge shale bracelet [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wraxall_Camp]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d24b6616-bd08-4edc-b385-cd5200383e9b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Between the forts of Glossop and Brough-on-Noe ran Doctor''s Gate, a Roman road named for Dr John Talbot, credited with improving its summit in the late 15th century.', 'A Roman road named after a 15th-century doctor — "gate" comes from the Scandinavian word for road. It connected two forts across the Peak District.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d24b6616-bd08-4edc-b385-cd5200383e9b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A Roman road named after a 15th-century doctor — "gate" comes from the Scandinavian word for road. It connected two forts across the Peak District.'' restored on Doctor''s Gate', '57a30aebcc0cb7187d55adf2274c76ae9054c08febf780879536e455673bfcf4|2d306249fc4992b92e932bc27bced5d019b2d94697af91a043b8a3dfc3040fb5|2d306249fc4992b92e932bc27bced5d019b2d94697af91a043b8a3dfc3040fb5', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Between the forts of Glossop and Brough-on-Noe ran Doctor''s Gate, a Roman road named for Dr John Talbot, credited with improving its summit in the late 15th century."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Doctor''s Gate ran between the forts at Glossop and Brough-on-Noe [S1]; Doctor''s Gate is a Roman road [S1]; it is named after Dr John Talbot [S2]; Dr John Talbot is credited with improving its summit section [S2]; that improvement dates to the late 15th century [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Doctor''s Gate is a Roman road [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doctor%27s_Gate]; It ran between the forts of Glossop (Melandra) and Brough-on-Noe (Navio) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doctor%27s_Gate]; It is named for Dr John Talbot [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doctor%27s_Gate]; Talbot is credited with improving its summit in the late 15th century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doctor%27s_Gate]; The road is in England (Derbyshire Peak District) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doctor%27s_Gate]; The road was named after Talbot (name recorded 1627 as Docto Talbotes Gate) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doctor%27s_Gate]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d28a0fac-0bfa-4c3f-a6c7-c616617e9286'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Also known as Castra Vetoniana, Pfünz Roman Fort in Bavaria was a Roman cohort camp. Before it, double V-shaped ditches were hewn from the rock, best preserved at the western rampart.', 'Built c. 90 AD on a 42-metre Jurassic hillspur in Bavaria. Double V-shaped ditches cut from bedrock survive. Part of the Rhaetian Limes -- Rome''s northern frontier, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d28a0fac-0bfa-4c3f-a6c7-c616617e9286:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "Built c. 90 AD on a 42-metre Jurassic hillspur in Bavaria. Double V-shaped ditches cut from bedrock survive. Part of the Rhaetian Limes -- Rome''s northern frontier, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site." restored on Pfünz Roman Fort', '058b1125a9ed0da91652a1e604290db3f04efeb6f5fcf8244dc475bba49a4492|f24ba3b96ef0107c1eaed7cdcc3438e505144924dca5da63252da4b8dd90525c|f24ba3b96ef0107c1eaed7cdcc3438e505144924dca5da63252da4b8dd90525c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Also known as Castra Vetoniana, Pfünz Roman Fort in Bavaria was a Roman cohort camp. Before it, double V-shaped ditches were hewn from the rock, best preserved at the western rampart."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pfünz Roman Fort is also known as Castra Vetoniana [S1]; it is in Bavaria [S1]; it was a Roman cohort camp [S1]; double V-shaped ditches lie in front of it [S2]; the ditches were hewn from the rock [S2]; the ditch at the western rampart is the best preserved [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pfünz Roman Fort was a Roman cohort camp [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pf%C3%BCnz_Roman_Fort]; It is in Bavaria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pf%C3%BCnz_Roman_Fort]; It is also known as Castra Vetoniana [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pf%C3%BCnz_Roman_Fort]; In front of the fort were double V-shaped ditches hewn from the rock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pf%C3%BCnz_Roman_Fort]; The ditch at the western rampart is the best preserved [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pf%C3%BCnz_Roman_Fort]; The ditches were V-shaped and double (two ditches) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pf%C3%BCnz_Roman_Fort]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d2968d12-d344-43d1-b192-ad3436cd38c1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the summit of Mynydd-y-Gaer, 300 m above sea level, a roughly circular bank and ditch mark Buarth-y-Gaer, an Iron Age hillfort or enclosure around a Bronze Age burial mound.', 'Summit enclosure at 300 metres on Mynydd-y-Gaer, defined by a single circular bank and ditch enclosing 1.1 hectares. A Bronze Age burial mound survives inside the walls.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d2968d12-d344-43d1-b192-ad3436cd38c1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Summit enclosure at 300 metres on Mynydd-y-Gaer, defined by a single circular bank and ditch enclosing 1.1 hectares. A Bronze Age burial mound survives inside the walls.'' restored on Buarth-y-Gaer', '9085f043c57baee98ebc00c0ba61abfeb1ed36589ba4d28f08b3e90c557464a0|e37fc4782c34a591e83abf68837c17f36a6cadb8629789c983e4d5852a925c6a|e37fc4782c34a591e83abf68837c17f36a6cadb8629789c983e4d5852a925c6a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the summit of Mynydd-y-Gaer, 300 m above sea level, a roughly circular bank and ditch mark Buarth-y-Gaer, an Iron Age hillfort or enclosure around a Bronze Age burial mound."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Buarth-y-Gaer is on the summit of Mynydd-y-Gaer [S1]; it lies 300 m above sea level [S1]; it is marked by a roughly circular bank and ditch [S4]; it is an Iron Age hillfort or enclosure [S1]; it encloses a Bronze Age burial mound [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Buarth-y-Gaer is an Iron Age hillfort or enclosure on the summit of Mynydd-y-Gaer [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buarth-y-Gaer]; The summit is 300 m above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buarth-y-Gaer]; A roughly circular bank and ditch mark the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buarth-y-Gaer]; The enclosure surrounds a Bronze Age burial mound [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buarth-y-Gaer]; The site is in Wales (Neath Port Talbot, South Wales) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buarth-y-Gaer]; The monument is considered an Iron Age hillfort, though possibly better termed an enclosure [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buarth-y-Gaer]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d2ecae04-608d-4755-9546-cf9ec07547af'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Known locally as the Giant''s Grave, Cohaw is a Neolithic double court tomb in County Cavan. When it was excavated in 1949, teeth, skull fragments and a child''s cremated remains emerged.', 'A Neolithic double court tomb with twin galleries and five burial chambers. Known locally as the "Giant''s Grave," it was originally two separate tombs joined together.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d2ecae04-608d-4755-9546-cf9ec07547af:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A Neolithic double court tomb with twin galleries and five burial chambers. Known locally as the "Giant\''s Grave," it was originally two separate tombs joined together.'' restored on Cohaw', '7c60203edb8435d1ee38513d3c17e0e932d17336626c8e442b73cc7312b7aa23|b2f7aa1b21446532859b2b2fbf2e0de19e69776b2525a83ba9c3c616f571e2d4|b2f7aa1b21446532859b2b2fbf2e0de19e69776b2525a83ba9c3c616f571e2d4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Known locally as the Giant''s Grave, Cohaw is a Neolithic double court tomb in County Cavan. When it was excavated in 1949, teeth, skull fragments and a child''s cremated remains emerged."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cohaw is known locally as the Giant''s Grave [S4]; Cohaw is a Neolithic double court tomb [S1]; it is in County Cavan [S1]; it was excavated in 1949 [S2]; the excavation found teeth and skull fragments [S3]; the excavation found the cremated remains of a child [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cohaw is a to