    ('d3da22e7-2499-4573-80f1-32037b641d57'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Raw Dykes in Leicester: two parallel Roman banks with a channel between, once at least 550 metres long, now 110 metres. In the English Civil War, Royalists set artillery on part of it.', 'A Roman earthwork with two parallel banks 20 m apart and a channel between them. Its original length was over 550 metres; pottery sherds from the early Roman period date the construction.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d3da22e7-2499-4573-80f1-32037b641d57:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A Roman earthwork with two parallel banks 20 m apart and a channel between them. Its original length was over 550 metres; pottery sherds from the early Roman period date the construction.'' restored on Raw Dykes', '2c0d9cf083ea7e6a3879175d54d3c2b564c3c351dc2a3dba325b8dc424506f98|d4f0b6016518469e5c7ea3d14be01fca7c071eea589c3af1bd274b53e2fcefc2|d4f0b6016518469e5c7ea3d14be01fca7c071eea589c3af1bd274b53e2fcefc2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Raw Dykes in Leicester: two parallel Roman banks with a channel between, once at least 550 metres long, now 110 metres. In the English Civil War, Royalists set artillery on part of it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Raw Dykes is in Leicester [S1]; it is Roman [S1]; it consists of two parallel banks [S2]; a channel runs between the banks [S2]; it was once at least 550 metres long [S3]; 110 metres of it survive today [S3]; during the English Civil War Royalists used part of it as an artillery emplacement [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Raw Dykes is an earthwork of two parallel banks with a channel between (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Dykes]; Raw Dykes is in Leicester [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Dykes]; The banks are Roman [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Dykes]; The banks are two and parallel [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Dykes]; There is a channel between the banks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Dykes]; It was originally at least 550 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Dykes]; Only 110 metres now survive [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Dykes]; In the English Civil War Royalists set artillery on part of it [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_Dykes]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d6ca1bdf-173c-4659-b1fc-c9fb7259c5a3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'West of Yeovil, Ham Hill holds evidence of occupation from the Mesolithic and Neolithic periods. Quarrying unearthed many artefacts, but destroyed much of the archaeological context.', 'Ham Hill''s golden hamstone — quarried here since Roman times — has built much of Somerset''s vernacular architecture. The hill itself is also a geological SSSI, a nature reserve, and a country park.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d6ca1bdf-173c-4659-b1fc-c9fb7259c5a3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description "Ham Hill''s golden hamstone — quarried here since Roman times — has built much of Somerset''s vernacular architecture. The hill itself is also a geological SSSI, a nature reserve, and a country park." restored on Ham Hill, Somerset', 'ce9c5b9a88a6c955034ede9818b4b2dd2119320f432c5036cfb3c3e33eef3965|3b052f201baedafd953aba5278b98ccff99b42c813a1406778e269d1ecddb768|3b052f201baedafd953aba5278b98ccff99b42c813a1406778e269d1ecddb768', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "West of Yeovil, Ham Hill holds evidence of occupation from the Mesolithic and Neolithic periods. Quarrying unearthed many artefacts, but destroyed much of the archaeological context."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-003 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ham Hill lies west of Yeovil [S1]; there is evidence of occupation from the Mesolithic and Neolithic periods [S4]; quarrying unearthed many artefacts [S2]; quarrying destroyed much of the archaeological context [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-007 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ham Hill is an archaeological site (hill fort and Roman site) in Somerset, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_Hill,_Somerset]; It lies west of Yeovil [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_Hill,_Somerset]; There is evidence of Mesolithic occupation [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_Hill,_Somerset]; There is evidence of Neolithic occupation [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_Hill,_Somerset]; Quarrying unearthed many artefacts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_Hill,_Somerset]; Quarrying destroyed much of the archaeological context [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham_Hill,_Somerset]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d6fabe8c-061f-4885-8e02-cc6276c6f23c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Despite its name, Cuddie Springs is no spring but an ephemeral lake. Its claypan fills after rainstorms and often takes months to dry, and so megafauna fossils survived there.', 'Palaeontological claypan on an ancient lake bed with the first unequivocal evidence of megafauna-human coexistence in Australia. Fossils survived because the lake refills and dries slowly after rain.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d6fabe8c-061f-4885-8e02-cc6276c6f23c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''Palaeontological claypan on an ancient lake bed with the first unequivocal evidence of megafauna-human coexistence in Australia. Fossils survived because the lake refills and dries slowly after rain.'' restored on Cuddie Springs', '7a5b0b4fc98129bb38c6d7b1facbba57f44c8ac6b8aee71de444892ad8e100a3|a661977fdddec8d74203bb2a7462c4132241f41d920e6cd4e62c8ce344d79cc0|a661977fdddec8d74203bb2a7462c4132241f41d920e6cd4e62c8ce344d79cc0', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Despite its name, Cuddie Springs is no spring but an ephemeral lake. Its claypan fills after rainstorms and often takes months to dry, and so megafauna fossils survived there."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "despite its name, Cuddie Springs is not a spring [S7]; Cuddie Springs is an ephemeral lake [S2, S7]; it has a claypan [S2]; the claypan fills with water after rainstorms [S3]; the claypan often takes months to dry [S3]; this filling and slow drying enabled fossils to survive there [S3]; the fossils there are megafauna fossils [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cuddie Springs is a site with megafauna fossils (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuddie_Springs]; Cuddie Springs is no spring [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuddie_Springs]; Cuddie Springs is an ephemeral lake [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuddie_Springs]; Its claypan fills after rainstorms [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuddie_Springs]; The claypan often takes months to dry [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuddie_Springs]; Megafauna fossils survived there [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuddie_Springs]; The fossils survived because of the slow drying of the claypan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuddie_Springs]; The fossils are of Australian megafauna [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuddie_Springs]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d7ef7408-c98b-4e69-a2fd-11c9beb0e144'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Ardotalia, a Roman fort near Glossop, was built by the First Cohort of Frisiavones with the 3rd Cohort of Bracara Augustani. Its name is a hypothetical emendation of Zerdotalia.', 'A Roman fort from the 1st-5th century AD, also known as Melandra Castle. It guarded a mountain pass and served as a checkpoint for travelers and trade goods.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d7ef7408-c98b-4e69-a2fd-11c9beb0e144:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A Roman fort from the 1st-5th century AD, also known as Melandra Castle. It guarded a mountain pass and served as a checkpoint for travelers and trade goods.'' restored on Ardotalia', '1a20ed6dda616bd5c5e1e42c0728b149db25e5b74bca601e74aa8c42ed1476a9|256d104c739e967be5a656ddb8aaed74f11bc1f7aff510cca0a03e5a9c2672aa|256d104c739e967be5a656ddb8aaed74f11bc1f7aff510cca0a03e5a9c2672aa', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ardotalia, a Roman fort near Glossop, was built by the First Cohort of Frisiavones with the 3rd Cohort of Bracara Augustani. Its name is a hypothetical emendation of Zerdotalia."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ardotalia is a Roman fort [S1]; it is near Glossop [S1]; it was built by the First Cohort of Frisiavones [S2]; the 3rd Cohort of Bracara Augustani helped build it [S5]; the name Ardotalia is a hypothetical emendation of Zerdotalia [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ardotalia is a Roman fort (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ardotalia]; It is near Glossop [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ardotalia]; It was built by the First Cohort of Frisiavones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ardotalia]; It was built with the 3rd Cohort of Bracara Augustani [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ardotalia]; Its name is a hypothetical emendation [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ardotalia]; The name emends Zerdotalia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ardotalia]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d83c15cf-679a-4e80-b138-344df541e860'::uuid, 'card_description', 'High on Monte Bàrbaro, 305 m above sea level, lie the ruins of Segesta, a major city of the Elymians of Sicily. On a hill just outside stands an unusually well-preserved Doric temple.', 'A 6th-century BC city of the Elymians, one of three indigenous peoples of Sicily. Its unfinished Doric temple stands roofless but otherwise remarkably intact.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d83c15cf-679a-4e80-b138-344df541e860:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A 6th-century BC city of the Elymians, one of three indigenous peoples of Sicily. Its unfinished Doric temple stands roofless but otherwise remarkably intact.'' restored on Segesta', '8541b7ccd348bdfd7c9187408ff34101b552643174c4651504fe0339d0b37e32|26a12c34c97416888e8a119ee56a89f73c74d5f4c29cc79e1fc3bc7785ac6105|26a12c34c97416888e8a119ee56a89f73c74d5f4c29cc79e1fc3bc7785ac6105', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "High on Monte Bàrbaro, 305 m above sea level, lie the ruins of Segesta, a major city of the Elymians of Sicily. On a hill just outside stands an unusually well-preserved Doric temple."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the ruins of Segesta lie on top of Monte Bàrbaro [S5]; they lie 305 m above sea level [S5]; Segesta was a major city of the Elymians [S1]; the Elymians were a people of Sicily [S1]; an unusually well-preserved Doric temple stands on a hill just outside the city [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-075 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Segesta is a (ruined) city of the Elymians of Sicily, one of the major Elymian cities [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segesta]; The ruins lie on top of Monte Bàrbaro [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segesta]; Monte Bàrbaro is 305 m above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segesta]; A temple stands on a hill just outside the ancient city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segesta]; The temple is Doric [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segesta]; The temple is unusually well preserved [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segesta]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d8dbc474-410c-4a59-8add-61fb14ede6a7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the east coast of the Malay Peninsula, Khao Sam Kaeo was an extremely important port, dated 400–100 BCE. Roman intaglios of yellow carnelian, carved with Roman gods, were found here.', 'A 400-100 BC port at the crossroads of the South China Sea and Bay of Bengal. Glass beads, stone ornaments, and metal tools reveal pan-Asian trade networks.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d8dbc474-410c-4a59-8add-61fb14ede6a7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''A 400-100 BC port at the crossroads of the South China Sea and Bay of Bengal. Glass beads, stone ornaments, and metal tools reveal pan-Asian trade networks.'' restored on Khao Sam Kaeo', 'ee4f31362203569de42659be5a5152f4becb14ce5a70d9b440089337e10f39df|4ca046b8a44025b81b31eba3cad5ea6278bb75d63858ddbdeb5297df05eefafc|4ca046b8a44025b81b31eba3cad5ea6278bb75d63858ddbdeb5297df05eefafc', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the east coast of the Malay Peninsula, Khao Sam Kaeo was an extremely important port, dated 400–100 BCE. Roman intaglios of yellow carnelian, carved with Roman gods, were found here."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Khao Sam Kaeo is on the east coast of the Malay Peninsula [S2, S4]; it was an extremely important port [S3]; it is dated 400-100 BCE [S3]; Roman intaglios were found there [S7]; the intaglios are made of yellow carnelian [S7]; the intaglios are carved with Roman gods [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-075 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Khao Sam Kaeo is an archaeological site (an ancient port settlement) on the east coast of the Malay Peninsula [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khao_Sam_Kaeo]; It was an extremely important port [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khao_Sam_Kaeo]; It is dated 400-100 BCE [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khao_Sam_Kaeo]; Roman intaglios were found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khao_Sam_Kaeo]; The intaglios are of yellow carnelian [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khao_Sam_Kaeo]; The intaglios are carved with Roman gods [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khao_Sam_Kaeo]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d9960a60-016d-4c3e-89ae-f3366abd45c7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Pinkuylluna, a mountain site northeast of Ollantaytambo, is named for the Pinkuyllu, an Andean flute. No one knows if flutes were played here, or if the name recalls the winds.', 'An Inca site on a mountainside between two rivers, facing the town of Ollantaytambo. The structures served as storehouses perched dramatically above the valley floor.', 'teaser-card-s025-rollback:d9960a60-016d-4c3e-89ae-f3366abd45c7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s025: card_description ''An Inca site on a mountainside between two rivers, facing the town of Ollantaytambo. The structures served as storehouses perched dramatically above the valley floor.'' restored on Pinkuylluna', 'f19e9e31cce7f7d9f423b6274fa294425b257d28f802aedc5658983eaa67ad49|1d9201df2b2dbeb756bebc99c6768435f976bd162dda380bc6f2b100649e0a27|1d9201df2b2dbeb756bebc99c6768435f976bd162dda380bc6f2b100649e0a27', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pinkuylluna, a mountain site northeast of Ollantaytambo, is named for the Pinkuyllu, an Andean flute. No one knows if flutes were played here, or if the name recalls the winds."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pinkuylluna is a site on a mountain [S1]; it lies northeast of Ollantaytambo [S2]; it is named for the Pinkuyllu [S3]; the Pinkuyllu is an Andean flute [S3]; it is unknown whether such flutes were played at the site [S4]; the name may instead refer to the local winds [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-075 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pinkuylluna is an archaeolo